e have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=2$ and ${x}_{3}=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-8+6 \\\\ -1+10-9 \\\\ 7+2-9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=2$ and ${x}_{3}=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186275386 :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $-6{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=0.$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- **III.** The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent." "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $-6{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=0.$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- **III.** The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent."] [:db/cas 17592186275386 :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-{a}_{3} & =-6[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12-8-4 \\\\ -6+4+2 \\\\ -12+7+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=6,\\,{x}_{2}=-1$ and ${x}_{3}=1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-{a}_{3} & =-6\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12-8-4 \\\\ -6+4+2 \\\\ -12+7+5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=6,\\,{x}_{2}=-1$ and ${x}_{3}=1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186275387 :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $-{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2}=0.$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n- **III.** The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent." "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $-{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2}=0.$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n- **III.** The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent."] [:db/cas 17592186275387 :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2} & =-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2} & =-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186275388 :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n- **III.** The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent." "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n- **III.** The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent."] [:db/cas 17592186275388 :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =4[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =4\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186275389 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $2y=3x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y & =3x \\\\ 2(0) & =3(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a+\\frac{3}{2}b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $2y=3x.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ k(\\frac{3}{2}a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $2y=3x.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x\\end{bmatrix},\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $2y=3x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y & =3x \\\\ 2(0) & =3(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a+\\frac{3}{2}b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $2y=3x.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ k(\\frac{3}{2}a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $2y=3x.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186275390 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ t \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2a+2b \\\\ a+b \\\\ (-a)+(-b)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2(a+b) \\\\ a+b \\\\ -(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k(2a) \\\\ ka \\\\ k(-a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2(ka) \\\\ ka \\\\ -(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ t \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2a+2b \\\\ a+b \\\\ (-a)+(-b)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2(a+b) \\\\ a+b \\\\ -(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k(2a) \\\\ ka \\\\ k(-a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2(ka) \\\\ ka \\\\ -(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186275391 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -2t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -2b \\\\ 3b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -2b \\\\ 3b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ (-2a)+(-2b) \\\\ 3a+3b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ -2(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ k(-2a) \\\\ k(3a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ -2(ka) \\\\ 3(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -2t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ -2b \\\\ 3b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ -2b \\\\ 3b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ (-2a)+(-2b) \\\\ 3a+3b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ -2(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ k(-2a) \\\\ k(3a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ -2(ka) \\\\ 3(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""] [:db/cas 17592186275392 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 3t \\\\ -2t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{3}=-\\frac{z}{2}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}4b \\\\ 3b \\\\ -2b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4b \\\\ 3b \\\\ -2b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4a+4b \\\\ 3a+3b \\\\ (-2a)+(-2b)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b) \\\\ -2(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{3}=-\\frac{z}{2}.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k(4a) \\\\ k(3a) \\\\ k(-2a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4(ka) \\\\ 3(ka) \\\\ -2(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{3}=-\\frac{z}{2}.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ 3t \\\\ -2t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{3}=-\\frac{z}{2}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}4b \\\\ 3b \\\\ -2b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4b \\\\ 3b \\\\ -2b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4a+4b \\\\ 3a+3b \\\\ (-2a)+(-2b)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b) \\\\ -2(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{3}=-\\frac{z}{2}.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k(4a) \\\\ k(3a) \\\\ k(-2a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4(ka) \\\\ 3(ka) \\\\ -2(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{3}=-\\frac{z}{2}.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""] [:db/cas 17592186275393 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r 