 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+2z & =3 \\\\ (4)-2(0)+2(1) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3+2y-2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+2z & =3 \\\\ (0)-2(0)+2(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $a=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3+1 \\\\ 0+0 \\\\ 0+1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+2z & =3 \\\\ (4)-2(0)+2(1) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] [:db/cas 17592186275420 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace.  In this case, we have that the vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\"." "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace.  In this case, we have that the vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\"."] [:db/cas 17592186275441 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186275442 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a line passing through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a line passing through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186275443 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186275444 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275444 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 7 & 13 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 7 & 13 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 2=24.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|24|=24.\n$$" "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 7 & 13 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 7 & 13 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 2=24.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|24|=24.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275445 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275445 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & -11 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & -11 & -2\\end{aligned}]=3\\cdot 3\\cdot 4\\cdot (-2)=-72.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-72|=72.\n$$" "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & -11 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & -11 & -2\\end{bmatrix}=3\\cdot 3\\cdot 4\\cdot (-2)=-72.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-72|=72.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275446 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275446 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]=7\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 5=-105.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-105|=105.\n$$" "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}=7\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 5=-105.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-105|=105.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275447 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275447 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 7 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 7 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]=(-3)\\cdot (-5)\\cdot 4\\cdot 3=180.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|180|=180.\n$$" "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 7 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 7 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=(-3)\\cdot (-5)\\cdot 4\\cdot 3=180.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|180|=180.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275448 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275448 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 12 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 12 & 5\\end{aligned}]=6\\cdot 2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 5=-180.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-180|=180.\n$$" "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 12 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 12 & 5\\end{bmatrix}=6\\cdot 2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 5=-180.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-180|=180.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275449 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275449 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $2$nd row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $3$rd row of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-2|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-6|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-6(4-25) \\\\  & =126.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|126|=126.\n$$" "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $2$nd row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $3$rd row of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-6\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-6(4-25) \\\\  & =126.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|126|=126.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275452 :question/problem "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275452 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}34 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{38}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 34\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}34 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{38}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 34\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275463 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane  $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane  $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275463 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}14 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275468 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275468 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $3$rd column of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =3|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 4\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-120\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-120|=120.\n$$" "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $3$rd column of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =3\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 4\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-120\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-120|=120.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275469 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275469 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]=3\\cdot 5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3=-45.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-45|=45.\n$$" "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=3\\cdot 5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3=-45.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-45|=45.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275470 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275470 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $1$st row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $2$nd row of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-2|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 4\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =8|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =8(-14+30) \\\\  & =128.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|128|=128.\n$$" "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $1$st row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $2$nd row of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 4\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8(-14+30) \\\\  & =128.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|128|=128.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186275571 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186275635 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 11 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 15 & 5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 11 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 15 & 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186275868 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 14 \\\\ 3 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 19 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 14 \\\\ 3 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 19 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186275891 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\