begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 13 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 22 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 27 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 35\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186277125 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186277140 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186277148 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186277277 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 21\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186277292 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186277320 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186277345 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 19 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 19 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186277354 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 21 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 21 \\\\ 26\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186277515 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186277612 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186277653 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186277724 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186278195 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $17a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $17a+\\frac{b}{c}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186278195 :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}17 & -34 \\\\ -34 & 68\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & -34 & 3 \\\\ -34 & 68 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}17 & -34 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{17}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{17}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{17} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n17a+\\frac{b}{c}=17(\\frac{3}{17})+\\frac{2}{1}=5.\n$$" "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}17 & -34 \\\\ -34 & 68\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}17 & -34 & 3 \\\\ -34 & 68 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}17 & -34 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{17}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{17}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{17} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n17a+\\frac{b}{c}=17(\\frac{3}{17})+\\frac{2}{1}=5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186278196 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n5{x}_{1}^{2}\\le 8{x}_{1}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =5{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 8{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =8({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=8(1)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 8.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $8.$ But before we conclude that $8$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=8.$\n\nNotice that when  $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =5{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =5(0)^{2}+8(1)^{2} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $8.$" "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n5{x}_{1}^{2}\\le 8{x}_{1}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =5{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 8{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =8({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=8(1)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 8.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $8.$ But before we conclude that $8$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=8.$\n\nNotice that when  $x={\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =5{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =5(0)^{2}+8(1)^{2} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $8.$"] [:db/cas 17592186278197 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{2}^{2}\\le 2{x}_{2}^{2},\\,-5{x}_{3}^{2}\\le 2{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=2(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 2.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $2.$ But before we conclude that $2$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=2.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2(1)^{2}-3(0)^{2}-5(0)^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $2.$" "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{2}^{2}\\le 2{x}_{2}^{2},\\,-5{x}_{3}^{2}\\le 2{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=2(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 2.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $2.$ But before we conclude that $2$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=2.$\n\nNotice that when $x={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2(1)^{2}-3(0)^{2}-5(0)^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186278198 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}^{2}\\le 4{x}_{1}^{2},\\,-7{x}_{2}^{2}\\le 4{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-7{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=4(1)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 4.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $4.$ But before we conclude that $4$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=4.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-7{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2(0)^{2}-7(0)^{2}+4(1)^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $4.$" "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}^{2}\\le 4{x}_{1}^{2},\\,-7{x}_{2}^{2}\\le 4{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-7{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=4(1)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 4.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $4.$ But before we conclude that $4$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=4.$\n\nNotice that when $x={\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-7{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2(0)^{2}-7(0)^{2}+4(1)^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186278199 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{2}^{2}\\ge -4{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-4{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -4{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=-4(1)=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge -4.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $-4.$ But before we conclude that $-4$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=-4.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-4{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-4(1)^{2}-3(0)^{2} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $-4.$" "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{2}^{2}\\ge -4{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-4{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -4{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=-4(1)=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge -4.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $-4.$ But before we conclude that $-4$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=-4.$\n\nNotice that when $x={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-4{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-4(1)^{2}-3(0)^{2} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $-4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186278200 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n6{x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 3{x}_{2}^{2},\\,4{x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 3{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge 3{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=3(1)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge 3.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $3.$ But before we conclude that $3$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=3.$\n\nNotice that when  $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =3(1)^{2}+6(0)^{2}+4(0)^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $3.$" "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n6{x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 3{x}_{2}^{2},\\,4{x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 3{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge 3{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=3(1)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge 3.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $3.$ But before we conclude that $3$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=3.$\n\nNotice that when  $x={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =3(1)^{2}+6(0)^{2}+4(0)^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186278252 :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$2$** | **$6$** | **$9$** | **$11$** | **$15$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$-5$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$3$** | **$5$** |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $4$ | $7$ | $9$ | $11$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear r