\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{uv} \\\\ u{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281389 :question/problem "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(3,0,-2)$ and $Q(1,1,-1).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ b \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(3,0,-2)$ and $Q(1,1,-1).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ b \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186281389 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the  line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=3+1+0=4.\n$$" "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the  line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=3+1+0=4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281390 :question/problem "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(0,-2,2)$ and $Q(1,-1,1).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ b \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(0,-2,2)$ and $Q(1,-1,1).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ b \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186281390 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+1+0=3.\n$$" "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+1+0=3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281391 :question/problem "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(-3,5,0)$ and $Q(0,-3,2).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(-3,5,0)$ and $Q(0,-3,2).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186281391 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the  line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\be