bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-3)-2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible and the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a triangle."] [:db/cas 17592186281417 :question/problem "![](images/95/957d55e3fb66bb2633798cf13d5c6049ffa4524ca585515ffaf88771ee32fe9b.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" "![](images/95/957d55e3fb66bb2633798cf13d5c6049ffa4524ca585515ffaf88771ee32fe9b.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$"] [:db/cas 17592186281417 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n